 ## Overview
-Froxel Volumetric Fog with Precomputed Lighting is a real-time rendering technique that simulates light scattering in participating media (mist, smoke, atmospheric fog) by dividing the camera view frustum into a 3D grid of voxels called "froxels" [S1, S2, unofficial]. Unlike traditional distance fog, which applies a uniform screen-space blur, this method allows for dynamic light beams, shadowed fog, and density variations within the volume [S2, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from its sibling method, [Froxel Volumetric Fog with Path-Traced Shadows](2314-froxel-volumetric-fog-with-path-traced-shadows.md), by using precomputed lighting passes (specifically Next Event Estimation approximations via compute shaders) rather than runtime path tracing for shadow calculation, offering a balance between visual fidelity and performance [S1, S2, unofficial].
+Froxel Volumetric Fog with Precomputed Lighting is a real-time rendering technique that simulates light scattering in participating media (mist, smoke, atmospheric fog) by dividing the camera view frustum into a 3D grid of voxels called "froxels" [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. Unlike traditional distance fog, which applies a uniform screen-space blur, this method allows for dynamic light beams, shadowed fog, and density variations within the volume [S2, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from its sibling method, [Froxel Volumetric Fog with Path-Traced Shadows](2314-froxel-volumetric-fog-with-path-traced-shadows.md), by using precomputed lighting passes (specifically Next Event Estimation approximations via compute shaders) rather than runtime path tracing for shadow calculation, offering a balance between visual fidelity and performance [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
 
